 "reading": "On HistData the rule keeps its sign and its strength before costs (gross month t +4.21) and the two feeds take the same side on 99.7 % of the days both trade, so the result is a property of gold's price path, not of the OANDA candles or fills. Over the same 2018 → 2026-10 days OANDA gives +0.050R net a trade (n 1682, month t +1.65, gross t +3.98) and HistData +0.042R (n 1603, month t +1.57, gross t +4.21); at 2x costs HistData −0.024R, OANDA −0.016R. On the 1593 common days the daily net R correlates 0.909 and the common-day means are +0.046R (HistData) and +0.063R (OANDA). By split: 2018-20 HistData +0.014R, OANDA +0.014R; 2021 → 2026-10 HistData +0.058R, OANDA +0.068R. The net month t on HistData over the window is +1.57: this check asks whether the feed carries the effect, not whether 2018-26 alone is an edge (the original's 2016-20 net was flat; the 2007-15 replication carries the evidence). HistData misses 115 days as too thin (fewer than 200 M5 candles), 112 of them in the file's 2023 hole (20 Feb to 28 Jul 2023, about 15 of every 21 hours present; XAU/USD 2023 is UNCERTIFIED in the clock audit research/edge/histdata-clock): the original's own thin rule skips them, so HistData has 79 fewer trades than OANDA, nearly all in 2023. Where both feeds trade, the fill candle is the same (median fill time difference 0 min), which also confirms the converted clock. The placebo on HistData: the real +0.042R beats 40 of 40 seeds (median −0.048R). Not an edge to trade and not a council rule; nothing live changes and no proposal is written."
